  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nThis morning...The marine layer remains about 1500 ft deep and low clouds\nare slowly developing and increasing in coverage over the coastal areas.\nLow cloud formation could be disrupted by mid-level debris clouds moving\nin from the southeast as mesoscale Convective Systems over AZ and northern\nMX collapse. However, SoCal is unlikely to see a repeat of the nocturnal\nconvection we saw Thursday night due to a less unstable environment.\n\nNumerical models are in good agreement through the middle of next\nweek with respect to the large-scale pattern. High pressure centered\nover the Inter-Mountain West will dominate our weather through \nMonday then a low pressure trough will deepen along the west \ncoast for the middle through the end of next week.\n\nAlthough temperatures will very slowly trend lower, daytime high \ntemperatures will remain about 5-10 degrees above normal for most \nareas through Monday while overnight low temps will remain 5-10 \ndegrees above normal only in the mountains and deserts. The \nmonsoonal flow will persist and continue to bring us significant \ndeep-layer moisture. This will maintain some chances for \nafternoon/evening showers/tstms through Tuesday. The marine layer\nwill remain shallow and the night and morning low clouds will \nmostly be restricted to the coastal areas.\n\nConditions begin to change more noticeably for the middle through\nthe end of next week as models consistently show a trough of low\npressure deepening along the west coast and displacing the high\npressure center as far to the southeast as the FL Panhandle by\nnext Friday. This will bring us cooler and drier conditions with a\ndeeper marine layer. By next Thursday, daytime high temps will be\nnear normal along the coast and 5-10 degrees below normal for\ninland areas. The below-normal temps will likely continue into\nnext weekend. The trough will bring dry southwest flow aloft, \nending the chances for pm showers/tstms by Wed. Low clouds and fog\ncould spread inland to cover most of the area west of the\nmountains by Thursday night.",
